---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const B = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

// Last review of this notice. Bump when the substance changes.
const lastReviewed = '2026-08-23';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="AI usage notice"
  description="How AI assistance is used in authoring extendedmatrix.org and the Extended Matrix documentation, in compliance with Article 50 of the EU AI Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689)."
>
  <section class="ai-usage">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="tag">AI usage</span>
      <h1>AI usage notice.</h1>
      <p class="ai-usage__prologue">
        The Extended Matrix Framework has been in continuous
        human-led development since 2015 — the year the EM formal
        language was first published. The tools that make it usable —
        EM Tools, 3D Survey Collection, s3Dgraphy and their peers —
        are the accreted product of a decade of code, review,
        teaching and fieldwork. This notice describes how AI
        assistance is used <em>on top of that base</em>, in line
        with Article 50 of the EU AI Act.
      </p>
      <p class="ai-usage__lede">
        The notice covers the authoring of
        <strong>extendedmatrix.org</strong> and of the Extended Matrix
        documentation hosted on Read the Docs, in line with the
        transparency framework of the EU Artificial Intelligence Act
        (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 — "AI Act"), and in particular
        Article 50 on transparency obligations for deployers of AI
        systems.
      </p>

      <p class="ai-usage__meta">
        Last reviewed: <time datetime={lastReviewed}>{lastReviewed}</time>.
      </p>

      <!-- ── Scope ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <section class="block">
        <h2>1 · Scope of this notice</h2>
        <p>
          This notice covers the content published on
          <strong>extendedmatrix.org</strong> (this website) and on the
          documentation subdomain <strong>docs.extendedmatrix.org</strong>,
          including the manuals of Extended Matrix (the formal language),
          EM Tools, 3D Survey Collection, Heriverse and related projects
          hosted on Read the Docs.
        </p>
        <p>
          It does <strong>not</strong> cover peer-reviewed articles and
          conference papers published under third-party editorial
          responsibility (journals, proceedings): those follow the AI
          disclosure policy of the respective publisher, which we honour
          at submission time.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ── How AI is used ───────────────────────────── -->
      <section class="block">
        <h2>2 · How AI tools are used in authoring</h2>
        <p>
          Large-language-model assistants (currently Anthropic's Claude,
          via the Claude Code and Claude Cowork interfaces) are used as
          an <em>assistive drafting and editing tool</em> in the
          authoring workflow of this site and of the manuals. Typical
          uses include drafting prose from bullet-point outlines,
          rephrasing existing text, cross-checking terminology across
          manuals, generating tabular summaries from source material,
          and helping enforce editorial constraints (length caps,
          heading structure, terminological consistency).
        </p>
        <p>
          AI is <strong>not</strong> used to publish content directly.
          Every text edit, whether produced ex novo or refined by the
          assistant, is reviewed by a human author before it is
          committed to the repository and pushed to the site. The
          commit history of the source repositories is the audit trail
          of that review.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ── Editorial responsibility ─────────────────── -->
      <section class="block">
        <h2>3 · Human review and editorial responsibility</h2>
        <p>
          Editorial responsibility for the content published on this
          site and on the Extended Matrix documentation is held by
          <strong>Emanuel Demetrescu</strong>, in personal research
          capacity, with the collaboration of the Extended Matrix
          contributors listed on the
          <a href={`${B}/team`}>team page</a>. Every publication
          decision — what to write, what to remove, what to endorse as
          the project's position — is made by a human author with
          subject-matter competence in the domains covered (Extended
          Matrix, stratigraphy, virtual reconstruction, 3D
          documentation, digital cultural heritage).
        </p>
        <p>
          This satisfies the two cumulative conditions of the
          human-review-and-editorial-responsibility exception provided
          for in <strong>Article 50(4) of the AI Act</strong>: an
          informed human deliberately examines the substance of the
          content before publication, and a human person holds the
          ultimate legal responsibility over its publication.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ── What we do not do ────────────────────────── -->
      <section class="block">
        <h2>4 · What this project does <em>not</em> do</h2>
        <ul class="ai-usage__list">
          <li>
            We do not deploy an AI chatbot or conversational agent that
            interacts directly with visitors: there is no LLM behind
            the scenes answering questions on the site. Any such tool,
            if introduced in the future, will be disclosed explicitly
            at the point of interaction, as required by
            Art. 50(1) of the AI Act.
          </li>
          <li>
            We do not publish AI-generated deepfake imagery, synthetic
            audio, or synthetic video, and we do not manipulate
            photographs of real people to represent events that did not
            occur (Art. 50(4), deepfake obligation).
          </li>
          <li>
            We do not use AI to generate content on matters of public
            interest (political, health, safety) outside of the
            project's technical and scientific scope, and we do not
            publish AI-drafted text without human review.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- ── Illustrations and figures ────────────────── -->
      <section class="block">
        <h2>5 · Illustrations, figures and screenshots</h2>
        <p>
          The vast majority of figures on this site and in the manuals
          are <strong>screenshots of real software</strong> (EM Tools
          in Blender, EMStudio, Heriverse, yEd), <strong>diagrams
          authored by the contributors</strong>, or
          <strong>photographs of real archaeological
          contexts</strong>. Where an illustration has been generated
          or substantially altered by an AI image tool, the caption
          says so explicitly.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ── Code ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <section class="block">
        <h2>6 · Code and software artefacts</h2>
        <p>
          The core libraries of the Extended Matrix Framework have
          been in continuous development for roughly a decade under
          human authorship. <strong>EM Tools</strong> (the Blender
          add-on) traces its first commits to <strong>2015</strong>,
          alongside the publication of the EM formal language;
          <strong>3D Survey Collection</strong> and
          <strong>s3Dgraphy</strong> consolidated later as
          independent components of the same framework. Every release
          on this decade-long line has been reviewed, cut and signed
          by a human maintainer with domain competence, and the
          public commit history of each repository is the audit trail.
        </p>
        <p>
          AI-assisted coding tools have been introduced more recently
          as an <em>additional productivity layer</em> on top of that
          established base — not as its foundation. Assisted
          contributions are integrated under the same maintainer
          discipline that has governed the framework since inception:
          every commit — assisted or not — is reviewed by a human
          maintainer before it is merged and released, and where
          relevant individual commits or pull requests carry a
          co-authorship trailer identifying the assistant. The AI
          Act's transparency obligations concern published
          <em>content</em>, not the internal development workflow of
          software; this section is included for completeness, not
          because it is required by Article 50.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ── Feedback and corrections ─────────────────── -->
      <section class="block">
        <h2>7 · Corrections and feedback</h2>
        <p>
          If you spot a factual error, a mis-attribution, or a passage
          that reads as AI-generated in a way that misrepresents the
          project's position, please write to
          <a href="mailto:emanuel.demetrescu@cnr.it">emanuel.demetrescu@cnr.it</a>
          or open an issue on the
          <a href="https://github.com/zalmoxes-laran/ExtendedMatrix-site/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">site repository</a>.
          The commit history of that repository is the public record of
          every editorial decision made on this site.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ── Regulatory references ────────────────────── -->
      <section class="block">
        <h2>8 · Regulatory references</h2>
        <ul class="ai-usage__list">
          <li>
            <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (AI Act)</a>
            — full text on EUR-Lex.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AI Act, Article 50</a>
            — transparency obligations for providers and deployers of
            certain AI systems.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/guidelines-ai-transparency-obligations" target="_blank" rel="noopener">European Commission — Guidelines on transparency obligations</a>
            (the interpretive guidance the Commission published in 2026).
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- ── Changes ──────────────────────────────────── -->
      <section class="block">
        <h2>9 · Changes to this notice</h2>
        <p>
          This notice may be updated when the authoring workflow
          changes (new tools introduced, new categories of content
          added, revised Commission guidance). The "last reviewed" date
          at the top of this page reflects the latest substantive
          update. The full revision history is available in the
          <a href="https://github.com/zalmoxes-laran/ExtendedMatrix-site/commits/main/src/pages/ai-usage.astro" target="_blank" rel="noopener">site repository</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .ai-usage { padding: 80px 0 60px; }
  .ai-usage h1 { margin: 14px 0 18px; }
  .ai-usage__prologue {
    max-width: 760px;
    color: var(--em-text);
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
    /* Subtle gold rule to mark the historical frame as a distinct
       moment before the legal-framing lede that follows. */
    border-left: 3px solid var(--em-gold);
    padding-left: 14px;
  }
  .ai-usage__lede {
    max-width: 760px;
    color: var(--em-text);
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }
  .ai-usage__meta {
    color: var(--em-text-muted);
    font-size: 13px;
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }
  .ai-usage .block { margin-bottom: 36px; max-width: 760px; }
  .ai-usage h2 { font-size: 19px; margin: 0 0 12px; color: var(--em-navy); }
  .ai-usage p { line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 12px; }
  .ai-usage a { color: var(--em-gold); }
  .ai-usage code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 13px;
    background: var(--em-navy-tint);
    color: var(--em-navy);
    padding: 1px 6px;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }
  .ai-usage__list {
    list-style: disc;
    padding-left: 22px;
    margin: 8px 0 16px;
  }
  .ai-usage__list li {
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
</style>